Yes — **if all of the conditions in CAR 401.19(1) are met**.

Under **CAR 401.19(1)**, a student pilot permit holder may act as pilot-in-command if:

- the flight is for the purpose of the holder’s **flight training**;
- the flight is conducted in **Canada**;
- the flight is conducted under **day VFR**;
- it is conducted under the **direction and supervision** of a person qualified to provide training toward the relevant permit, licence, or rating; and
- **no passenger is carried on board**.

So, for a **daytime VFR training flight in Canada with no passengers**, the answer is **yes**, provided the supervision requirement in **401.19(1)(d)** is also satisfied.

Also, **CAR 401.19(2)** adds that if the student pilot permit holder is enrolled in an **integrated course**, they may act as pilot-in-command during a flight conducted under **day or night VFR**.

Yes, **for the purposes of the student pilot permit privilege in CAR 401.19**, a holder enrolled in an **integrated course** may act as pilot-in-command during a flight conducted under **day or night VFR**. See **CAR 401.19(2)**, which creates an exception to **401.19(1)(c)**.

However, the other conditions in **CAR 401.19(1)** still apply:
- the flight must be for the purpose of the holder’s flight training;
- it must be conducted in Canada;
- it must be under the direction and supervision of a qualified person;
- and **no passenger may be carried on board**.

Also, **CAR 401.43** says a night rating allows the holder of a permit or licence to exercise its privileges by night, but the specific student pilot permit authority comes from **CAR 401.19(2)**.

I don’t have enough information in the available regulations to say whether any additional operating rules would apply to the particular aircraft or operation beyond these provisions.

Yes, **if the conditions in CAR 401.32(1) are met**. Under **CAR 401.32(1)**, the holder of a **multi-crew pilot licence — aeroplane** may act as **co-pilot** of a **turbine-powered aeroplane** operated **day or night** under **VFR, VFR OTT, or IFR** if the aeroplane:

- is a **transport category aircraft**;
- has a **minimum flight crew document** specifying a minimum flight crew of **two pilots**;
- is of a **type endorsed** on the holder’s licence; and
- is operated under **subpart 4 of Part VI or under Part VII**.

So, **yes**, but only if those conditions are satisfied.
